  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A linear chandelier is held up by NNN supports. The ithi^{th}ith support initially holds up a weight WiW_iWi​, and will collapse if the weight on it is **more than or equal to** AiA_iAi​. Note that initially, Wi&lt;AiW_i \\lt A_iWi​&lt;Ai​ for all 1≤i≤N1 \\le i \\le N1≤i≤N.\n\nWhenever Wi≥AiW_i\\ge A_iWi​≥Ai​ for some support, the following happens:\n\n\nThe ithi^{th}ith support is destroyed;\nLet xxx and yyy be uniformly random non-negative integers such that x+y=Wix+y=W_ix+y=Wi​.\n\nAdd xxx to Wi−1W_{i-1}Wi−1​ and add yyy to Wi+1W_{i+1}Wi+1​.\nNote that:\n\n\nIf either one of the neighbours do not exist, all the weight is added to the remaining neighbour.\nIf both neighbours do not exist, the weight dissipates.\nFor all 1≤i≤N1 \\le i \\le N1≤i≤N, find the **minimum** weight you need to add to WiW_iWi​, so that there is a nonzero chance all supports end up destroyed.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input contains a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nThe first line of each test case contains NNN, the number of supports.\nThe second line of each test case contains NNN integers W1,W2,…,WNW_1, W_2, \\ldots, W_NW1​,W2​,…,WN​, denoting initial weight on  each support.\nThe third line of each test case contains NNN integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​, denoting the minimum load at which a support destroys.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test, output NNN space-separated integers. The ithi^{th}ith of these should be the **minimum weight** you need to add to the ithi^{th}ith chandelier, so that there is a nonzero chance that all supports end up destroyed.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1041 \\leq T \\leq 10^41≤T≤104",
      "1≤N≤3⋅1051 \\leq N \\leq 3\\cdot 10^51≤N≤3⋅105",
      "1≤Wi&lt;Ai≤1091 \\leq W_i \\lt A_i \\leq 10^91≤Wi​&lt;Ai​≤109",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 3⋅1053\\cdot 10^53⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "4\n3\n1 1 1\n2 2 2\n1\n1\n1000000000\n2\n1 1234\n2 5678\n7\n122 179 269 184 250 104 455\n398 203 318 340 312 489 464",
        "output": "1 1 1\n999999999\n4443 4444\n276 146 49 156 291 385 9",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: The following is possible if we add weight 111 to support 222:\n\nSupport 222 collapses, since W2=2≥2W_2=2\\ge 2W2​=2≥2. A weight x=1x=1x=1 gets added to W1W_1W1​, and a weight y=1y=1y=1 gets added to W3W_3W3​.\nSupport 111 collapses, since W1=2≥2W_1=2\\ge 2W1​=2≥2.\nSupport 333 collapses, since W3=2≥2W_3=2\\ge 2W3​=2≥2.\n\nIf we add zero weight, then nothing would happen and all supports would stay standing. So the answer for i=2i=2i=2 is 111.\nTest case 222: We have to add 999999999999999999999999999 weight to the only support to make it collapse.\nTest case 333: Note that we could add a weight of 111 to support 111 to make it collapse, but then, there would be no way to make the second support collapse. We can show that we have to add at least 444344434443 weight to support 111 to make it possible for both supports to end up destroyed."
      }
    ]
  },
